d #edb/ref 17592186389181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659a692b-2aae-5acc-a235-560ceb57bafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389183} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c0fc1d-78bf-56a3-bddf-42bd659ad7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389184} {:answer/id #uuid "404681f6-e3e2-5a85-a1a6-1e1e580bf865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5889bd5e8d537757de25a7b1a868ff8a903da14c9088c5f8de5a70d8b078552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389185} {:answer/id #uuid "121673a3-bbfa-573d-a9d1-f20fe0e8e765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389186} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e134b4-7103-5125-b7fc-086313478261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b0171dfd42295e39cd45186e8edba3a5105fbd1bd02a1621223091de75815f61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121673a3-bbfa-573d-a9d1-f20fe0e8e765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389186} :answer-field/id #uuid "3825278d-de78-5afb-82f2-adfd041f336b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2f0da55-1926-59a4-96c8-e68855af7a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161837" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773f7224-683a-5657-93fd-0b49bba9ddc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389190} {:answer/id #uuid "3223453f-3db3-5cf7-9170-0776a21f7b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c24c3-b6bf-58dc-bb07-e1245dcf6b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389192} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae8e4b1-ec09-543b-b30d-54e69ad80dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/105e6e8a010b7b9001f75cf17e11cbbc1e031d83cbc2ddfafc3595c745106f3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389193} {:answer/id #uuid "682bc6d2-be17-5ccb-842c-4b844ea68ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/090bab0ac1c6356010d83a6ad54c59b53b7dd12062fb5e331a936ce3f3a1b411.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3223453f-3db3-5cf7-9170-0776a21f7b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd81eba-4d1a-5e26-b6d5-47144737edcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389189}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99972da5-e4e5-5505-b6d1-ad2a467bcb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-161839" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2407dcd5-06ff-568b-bb6d-b2494e2bd942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389197} {:answer/id #uuid "07712d2e-2b92-521c-b6ae-d43d00f464d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389198} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3ba601-6388-5b1c-ac01-5522063f4425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fd2a51ca3a1654c2e857018268bf251366472a2c5d7f90009d5ccfbf1b1edd9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389199} {:answer/id #uuid "dc797ebb-78cb-5a5b-ac03-97f75fe59dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389200} {:answer/id #uuid "2201cc6c-a008-5122-982f-2699a2c582b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95bf5e059fda0fc1ba8423b35fffa57d9cae0cc2a2a7e40b0c2539b6a7ae7678.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2407dcd5-06ff-568b-bb6d-b2494e2bd942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389197} :answer-field/id #uuid "644861a4-b1bb-5e40-b887-ad0f73e8386a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389196}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82d50c5e-c224-54bc-84fa-f5633f671803" :question/math-academy-id "q-161840" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44718c67-0ba1-524e-9c98-36d26b5e3a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389204} {:answer/id #uuid "225446d3-d261-5650-bed9-eaacbb9f2ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389205} {:answer/id #uuid "f7224ff9-23ce-5fb3-a58f-396aece4ce79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fd2a51ca3a1654c2e857018268bf251366472a2c5d7f90009d5ccfbf1b1edd9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389206} {:answer/id #uuid "133f4c43-8534-5694-8789-da248d6d37e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389207} {:answer/id #uuid "83b86f4d-ddaa-5fd0-acc9-387f47e13895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0b764651dbc7999e7af13214685796b5045fab89f101bbc72bf413112df3c51f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "225446d3-d261-5650-bed9-eaacbb9f2ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389205} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f005f2-f2a3-59bb-859f-a90699b333ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389203}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4aa2b6c-6ba5-59e1-9b2a-ad9aa14c6d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161841" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ -v & -u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-u)-(-v)\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & >0 \\\\ v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,3)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72f242ce-c65f-5c89-b0bc-7d65d210918f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389211} {:answer/id #uuid "62f79a35-0524-561a-ba91-d11cf1227837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d944eb13437b86c0cf6ae4fa8bca09031ed0f2f85a9f20bcb0050a6de9f8ebb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389212} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa99b0c-e94e-5958-a986-2c13e020da52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389213} {:answer/id #uuid "acff9e64-428c-54e8-8971-9f328584d347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389214} {:answer/id #uuid "2b75873a-335e-5bb0-9312-2f0d35d3633e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7bc22ac4dfd2d7015952144ece63f91ecccef2a992649aece60dc8a70f1d018.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfa99b0c-e94e-5958-a986-2c13e020da52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389213} :answer-field/id #uuid "317c3736-80dd-5537-ba65-91575de09aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e552dcc6-87b8-5585-84f4-2c455a9099f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161856" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133642 :question/id #uuid "132af6fa-454c-596c-a057-dfe531792102" :question/math-academy-id "e-6283" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,\\sqrt 2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) =t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k},$ then along the curve we have $x = t^2,$ $y = t,$ and $z=1.$  \n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t\\cdot 1}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u= 4t^2+1,$ $\\textrm d u=8t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[{9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[27-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 26 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da23f1c0-678a-51c0-a2db-e12d756f9120" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e37f47-83d1-5088-9b85-0e2e24287bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342305} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac14869-5cac-504f-add3-8e9d269fda7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342328} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae148b-fb50-5f08-a2c0-8a72efe39137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343656} {:answer/id #uuid "a2572c76-84e6-542d-8804-715f0e7d0f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343665} {:answer/id #uuid "eed4f8df-a5a6-55f1-8a77-0e82d4221bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed4f8df-a5a6-55f1-8a77-0e82d4221bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344337} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f0f7cf8-f8e0-5481-a04a-8c128f9e6dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a42a0c64-6afa-5778-a107-25576497d815" :question/math-academy-id "q-76186" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\,$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t+1)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=t+1.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{(t+1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{3/2}{|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & ={25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2} \\\\  & =125-27 \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4206dd2-7f0d-5a39-81ac-42f0f7afd440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133111} {:answer/id #uuid "bef21a17-bc41-51cb-bd6f-ccfb84019bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133112} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e019f4-70de-5a92-a4ca-84b324f58dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133113} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9f6d66-d570-59cd-b849-c86b67cd5b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133114} {:answer/id #uuid "721bd124-318f-520e-bd5c-2af024eb5c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133115}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc97926-178c-5cd1-8a7d-3f12cff0ecfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133110}] :question/id #uuid "04dc89d7-54ea-5f6e-88f3-7fb468d7a9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76187" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4yz\\cos xds$, where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = ti + \\sin tj + k$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c74ebde-4483-557d-bc7f-96d2eae35d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281803} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f1600-c667-5053-87bf-a32e6404cd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} {:answer/id #uuid "8298ffad-58f2-5c03-8c84-d9f00f0346bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7(2\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282818} {:answer/id #uuid "c84c1cb8-787a-5d55-a741-7cdf8092bf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283442} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bd9128-7071-5f60-80ca-f9fcc77741a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f1600-c667-5053-87bf-a32e6404cd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c701af0-373b-5ef2-ba21-ba5e8d9c684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284116}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2dda2ab-f3d6-5a31-a33f-5b15f55a34c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167612" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=3t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j\\,$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=3t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y={t}^{3}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =27{t}^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =28{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}28{t}^{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{4},$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}21\\cdot {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =14({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec86e50-8126-5c8c-9c9e-7b518ae7992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342364} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8f2e2d-51c7-5375-9e3c-dedb16da721e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343311} {:answer/id #uuid "a735d400-c4c3-527f-96ac-bc42ae20a5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343710} {:answer/id #uuid "adb27abf-79df-56ff-bf2b-b4946023e074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343766} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75af4f4-1064-571f-8598-a6fa76be3720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d73cf87-1a19-5cca-88b7-764818d04cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76479" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0<t<\\sqrt{\\pi /2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,j-2t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4b14bf-da94-5587-9476-c6c9f1763513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342466} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd66d9f-895b-589b-a593-4fcc58493314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342486} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d5513-4913-5c07-b97e-b502cd923def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342600} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5cec4d-eafc-5470-8930-282ed398fa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e475247-06f5-5122-b5e7-edfc19026889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344627}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ffd2389-f864-5810-9cd3-9a41b1f9dc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-76484" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5},$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$   \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =5|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t| \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =25{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =25\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b563221-3548-560c-9fc8-b290090582fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342472} {:answer/id #uuid "34a3d682-f094-5b8e-9966-be8b9fc8ad8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342675} {:answer/id #uuid "618e4548-1271-521c-9a14-4e3337de95f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343065} {:answer/id #uuid "cd843b1d-6720-54f4-98b0-8e4c42afe883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344048} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf920228-2dc2-55b3-a8f5-efbc9659d941" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "004b67de-a618-52fc-b8e0-1a45d41d2be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76496" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Using a Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133643 :question/id #uuid "25d6bb34-eeb6-5bb7-8b66-0e6424244eeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6285" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y \\,\\mathbf i + (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = (1+2t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (1-t)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r (t))\\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = 1 + 2t, \\qquad y = 1 - t .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+((1+2t)+(1-t))\\,j \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+(t+2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((1-t)\\,i+(t+2)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i-j) \\\\  & =(1-t)\\cdot 2+(t+2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-2t-t-2 \\\\  & =-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a9183a-68b4-5bad-b2d8-3bcf6597f263" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35328e91-1ef1-5761-9a3c-7f67ec2a2ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257250} {:answer/id #uuid "3b66565f-5714-54a9-85c4-0ff27d7118fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257312} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0c7184-545c-5854-8b44-bd9cc2b4d287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257584} {:answer/id #uuid "682aa2b5-99c5-55c1-864f-3d951936c921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257703} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0611e-d7f4-59c9-b753-ef6a1ce7a6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35328e91-1ef1-5761-9a3c-7f67ec2a2ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257250} :answer-field/id #uuid "c017c03d-1ac2-5b93-9628-5f5c0ad12c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259366}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3df2ce8-a374-5f4f-80bf-b8dbf37e4ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124694" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that  $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}\\,i+2t\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =1+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+4-(1+1)] \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b6f0d80-4352-5158-8f93-1840b1e6324a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324725} {:answer/id #uuid "64abec96-fecf-52bf-b665-465ae20f4135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324804} {:answer/id #uuid "95deb8cb-eec5-56d6-b7c7-f205853e1f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325249} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9661da-4c00-5003-8d44-16815092e8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325327} {:answer/id #uuid "b603e0fd-9b34-5f6e-b31b-3b2140f17ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{109}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b603e0fd-9b34-5f6e-b31b-3b2140f17ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{109}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325535} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca3dfc2b-fd60-5b79-a0ca-b2bb5282bb22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326587}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7cef591-bf6c-502e-a297-4a0f671dac2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39340" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+(x+1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=3t\\,i+(2+t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=2+t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={x}^{2}y\\,i+(x+1)\\,j \\\\  & =(3t)^{2}(2+t)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}(2+t)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =(18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{3})\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{3})\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i+j) \\\\  & =54{t}^{2}+27{t}^{3}+3t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}54{t}^{2}+27{t}^{3}+3t+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[18{t}^{3}+\\frac{27}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(18+\\frac{27}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{109}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166e92fc-f76a-54b5-a0dd-63e74ebd5717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256986} {:answer/id #uuid "25c15736-d6ba-56e2-90f2-ba5a0588cc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257104} {:answer/id #uuid "911d4916-bdd8-561f-b96d-1989bde4de80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258064} {:answer/id #uuid "d636aee9-1a04-5a63-b2bd-90b7b476ffb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258661} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c9c31a-af3d-5b26-a0c9-8f70bbe49d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "911d4916-bdd8-561f-b96d-1989bde4de80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258064} :answer-field/id #uuid "3395a8a4-72d7-5431-a3bf-dbf9b39ab8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ff38139-bd4b-55b1-a4ac-c9a0d7dde49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124688" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that  $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(y-1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+(y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(-1-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a1cc1e-6c7c-5eb5-8782-6185aaada67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257132} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5c1e37-56f1-50fb-b6d1-e94b31278780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257462} {:answer/id #uuid "6934487a-8eb1-5c6b-9b99-2f712e3ce1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257716} {:answer/id #uuid "c098ca54-0767-55d1-a79d-5dd92229c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258464} {:answer/id #uuid "e724745c-f49d-514b-9248-b9b3caf11eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a1cc1e-6c7c-5eb5-8782-6185aaada67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257132} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0b360f-7b50-508f-bc77-f1e69e16c0d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259059}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aaea8c36-ce4d-54ba-a2eb-6aed8473ac11" :question/math-academy-id "q-124689" :question/problem "Evaluate${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt \\\\  & =t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248ddbce-6070-5712-afac-3351be818687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257089} {:answer/id #uuid "650b1bbb-fae3-5145-829f-a04807f5d14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257669} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3dfb4b-820b-5b12-a401-88c1479f2704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258168} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e142f3-f380-597f-a2ec-0f4c63ea82f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258898} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbc0191-1988-5241-a6d2-3448d950e37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650b1bbb-fae3-5145-829f-a04807f5d14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257669} :answer-field/id #uuid "c11f27a1-5eba-5c1b-9677-29d5ce54bfe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259370}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18a06bdc-cb94-54a8-9fb9-6d1d6e3f27c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124691" :question/problem "Evaluate  ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot ((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t{cos}^{2}⁡t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }t\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aaf5016-f079-5a89-acf7-5d9a6c81a79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257434} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad06c35-e2d5-5c92-9374-c0ee3dc71543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257435} {:answer/id #uuid "e008728e-7840-5a56-a663-2a5cf58d4b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258734} {:answer/id #uuid "f192773f-28fe-5dff-8add-6f7c44d632d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258885} {:answer/id #uuid "f2eb3173-51d7-535e-aaa5-a8f0959fd452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad06c35-e2d5-5c92-9374-c0ee3dc71543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257435} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c9f6c2-e852-5e7b-bee8-30f1a7b46854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259461}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b8d179c-8a3b-5f0a-ae95-7266bd00dc49" :question/math-academy-id "q-124693" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=y\\,i+x\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[1-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ad2868-cfa8-5e70-a096-102e472ce7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256798} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0343e0-516c-5453-92f1-46fc15399f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257439} {:answer/id #uuid "8e941010-5b37-5c05-b385-e2206c35e82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258037} {:answer/id #uuid "a9298b81-fd3f-5dc0-9c39-af3414a2639f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258257} {:answer/id #uuid "a9acd370-6aed-5cc9-b5fe-f4f50c42aa0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ad2868-cfa8-5e70-a096-102e472ce7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256798} :answer-field/id #uuid "573a9156-a300-5f27-97b1-a6a93a7b47a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cfcca7d-9fbd-5623-b577-ba72ef3dd747" :question/math-academy-id "q-124696" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [3,4].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-1)+1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2({t}^{2}-1)}{t-1}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-2(t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (2t\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot 2t-2(t+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-2(t+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =[-16+9] \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9c9635-514f-5437-89a3-2311516df929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324616} {:answer/id #uuid "542f73e6-a0ee-5f63-a36b-e67fde3a6341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324669} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa4c2bd-e1e8-5ef1-b567-38d573db5658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324998} {:answer/id #uuid "98e23fb5-b473-5394-a5ba-4656c5c07d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325270} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e0be4-5f46-5ddf-98a2-9f9d363b7d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e0be4-5f46-5ddf-98a2-9f9d363b7d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323} :answer-field/id #uuid "526c7e14-1815-5fc1-a1ad-f65ad4c3be74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326361}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d6ca191-15db-5023-b8de-7e13937e634b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39422" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,dt \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-2(\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals of a Vector-Valued Functions Over Plane Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133644 :question/id #uuid "f07dd3d3-606f-520f-a95e-04303aa32f24" :question/math-academy-id "e-6307" :question/problem "Find an integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f53d78debfec5f8c50e0f3a5947d2a36126e18c073d7b0cc4c59d506753059b3.png)\n\nThe path of integration is shown above. We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $f(x,y) =  xy^3$. Along the path, we have $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t,$ and so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3} \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int^{\\pi/2}_{0} \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, \\textrm{d}t .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f7479a-a84c-59fa-a7ea-9377c7e65b19" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e006fbf-73db-5714-80d3-da70e1961752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324338} {:answer/id #uuid "4454bda2-b710-5b8e-962a-af8f72db9bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324524} {:answer/id #uuid "969d3d60-bae9-5b94-bb58-6e2e78279810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325255} {:answer/id #uuid "dc88ce8a-6d69-5cce-ae62-6e652a14c027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325896} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fc0716-e381-59ad-99c7-f5348e2706dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e006fbf-73db-5714-80d3-da70e1961752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324338} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa3112f0-f6c4-537c-b0c9-7b639ee76cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326527}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13f50e74-e351-581a-98f5-c8e27aa5380d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40253" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized \n by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+t,$ and $y={t}^{2}-t.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t+1)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4t+1+4{t}^{2}-4t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2}[({t}^{2}+t)-({t}^{2}-t)] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\sqrt{2}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f149a5c8-374a-5613-9e94-f7a85f4e3578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}(8t^{3} + t - 2)dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133117} {:answer/id #uuid "07f01a2c-6102-5f57-9a81-827ca9995533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}(4t^{3} - 3)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133118} {:answer/id #uuid "0e53f525-69b5-5819-9c6b-7b1f40a1280e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}(t^{3} - t^{2} + 4t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133119} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8c7275-20d2-58af-b910-a3018cd7a77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}(8t^{3} - t^{2} + 4t - 4)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133120} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb682b0-7b0d-59e5-8435-a35378e856c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}(8t^{3} + t^{2} - 4)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133121}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee84ab5-a5cf-5da3-83b7-b25c4dfcdc31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133116}] :question/id #uuid "2f8d1c23-74d2-5539-9285-abeec9fd6ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76368" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{3} - y)ds$, where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 2ti + (2 - t)j$ for $-1 \\le t \\le 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263de2e3-27ae-52e2-8582-4dfb39ba6ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282023} {:answer/id #uuid "75614e75-3308-50a7-a253-f1f9b76e3296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2t+1}}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282694} {:answer/id #uuid "76433ca0-b7f8-577e-a495-c0ae272242e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282701} {:answer/id #uuid "89172764-a6f4-5672-8136-d35db577b74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(2t+1)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282872} {:answer/id #uuid "8caffd9e-930e-5e4b-b97e-bcbc15c29c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76433ca0-b7f8-577e-a495-c0ae272242e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282701} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee764035-aa19-5d16-9448-a4b9dd4171f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284356}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bb4ae8a-0b75-5e62-bc9b-0900b739960b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{4y+1}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372a5048-17c2-5076-9635-4ff4ad531da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342693} {:answer/id #uuid "60710012-7ccb-54d9-aac8-7c21a4db39de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343056} {:answer/id #uuid "7143c54a-f0e9-502c-b646-975daaf14a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343209} {:answer/id #uuid "77d755de-6003-5532-a85f-2d9e8cb137d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}-2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343274} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c1d1a-5321-50f7-aff8-7b81e9c745ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60710012-7ccb-54d9-aac8-7c21a4db39de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343056} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d47b24e-5186-5a2c-bc62-0a4991b4b99e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344569}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e477990a-d15e-595b-a078-39c7382f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-76377" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2},$ $y=t-1,$ and $z=t+1.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({t}^{2})-(t-1)^{2}-(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-({t}^{2}-2t+1)-({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}+2t-1-{t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2(2{t}^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f72ef384-5e6e-52c5-839e-3beee185405e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393620} {:answer/id #uuid "42147aee-d1b6-54a9-a34c-6883f2cfc5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393621} {:answer/id #uuid "38308454-7a76-5601-988c-cc81a2d49d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}-t)\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393622} {:answer/id #uuid "9d940168-caf2-55dc-abc9-6478a8eb7fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393623} {:answer/id #uuid "89f9a881-2016-5d9d-b0c2-417ca0dbf7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42147aee-d1b6-54a9-a34c-6883f2cfc5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393621} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0d4305-f486-5b23-8a23-7975fef766e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393619}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9dd51c2a-c921-500f-b456-d9efa51cbc02" :question/math-academy-id "q-76356" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+1,$ $y={t}^{2}-1,$ and $z={t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j+2t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({t}^{2}+1)+({t}^{2}-1)-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa31ddbb-d2dc-5e24-b119-3e57906a0e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393627} {:answer/id #uuid "6e191add-76d4-52b0-a4d0-b55782dad06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393628} {:answer/id #uuid "e78b94bc-a9b3-5508-8e70-1864ef024d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393629} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7e2485-49a8-54d2-9942-acfa1e262b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393630} {:answer/id #uuid "1e167558-c450-5d8c-885f-dd7f2edd9925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e191add-76d4-52b0-a4d0-b55782dad06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393628} :answer-field/id #uuid "80947165-9047-5b9a-b812-2946d21a42e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393626}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfad2296-6fbf-598f-a4ed-63e51e9c1622" :question/math-academy-id "q-76379" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function as a Definite Integral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133645 :question/id #uuid "8bf6a863-9b9f-5877-84bc-3e11d71261c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6308" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y=4t.$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =xy,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{2}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20{[{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c13ee6c3-92c9-5a3d-b1c3-cc0e6dc9c11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{96}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131702} {:answer/id #uuid "dd83dc0f-ae75-597e-9bf5-97ca5882c46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131703} {:answer/id #uuid "23b2b615-b99c-50e0-a324-227c7235fe42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{104}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131704} {:answer/id #uuid "442792c7-4dbd-5bc7-83a1-8c1fbcc8e6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131705} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3b94ad-f1e1-5a06-b9fd-7ea4ca4724ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131706}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ae85dffa-91e2-5047-afe6-7098bbee9ba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131701}] :question/id #uuid "b920de5e-825e-5dcf-9aac-175e4a51e8a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40258" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = 3x$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t) = ⟨\\frac{4t^{3/2}}{3}, \\frac{t^{2}}{2} - t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "867e1617-e755-5f5e-a7fd-c9f0fce495d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54(1 + π^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132068} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a0a023-4af9-5c74-84ad-835ca54417c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54π^{2}(1 + 2π^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132069} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4c3273-3648-5e4d-a894-45f38532de5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27(1 + 2π^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132070} {:answer/id #uuid "1b35ab1c-0cdc-51c6-a919-098edbb8b942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18π^{2}(1 + 2π^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132071} {:answer/id #uuid "2640c3d9-c841-5fee-a9ae-5536944db3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54(1 + π^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132072}] :answer-field/id #uuid "edacb75a-137f-5143-b6be-80a383127ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132067}] :question/id #uuid "38682a53-16b6-5629-9f8b-b56796ac3444" :question/math-academy-id "q-41160" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds$, where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t) = 3(\\cos t + t\\sin t)i + 3(\\sin t - t\\cos t)j$ for $0 \\le t \\le 2π$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48320d50-11dc-5c25-b211-1a6293887c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} {:answer/id #uuid "667ecc69-8180-56b1-a381-85cc0e7c7286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282577} {:answer/id #uuid "7b621e50-9338-5847-b8c4-30f5e5589d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282748} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1007a-4679-560a-84f7-593c87ce0e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282941} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac19e51-1f63-5f51-98b2-8211bafdd295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48320d50-11dc-5c25-b211-1a6293887c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} :answer-field/id #uuid "69141054-77da-5506-94da-51c42eb49c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284109}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72644873-b2ba-57a8-bd4c-d5dc5e8e5941" :question/math-academy-id "q-167609" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$    \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t+(1+t)=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1+1-0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10206875-a5c4-5022-9ee9-43b3a21b42b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281836} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd849f2-6a5c-5409-a292-f59a3dc7a36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282215} {:answer/id #uuid "4544a980-713e-5e34-893c-6b08076a1997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282275} {:answer/id #uuid "7f63b4af-1a8d-5157-bbb0-dc8287c37b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282786} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a5f08d-e51f-5026-8bb2-63d5b7342f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f63b4af-1a8d-5157-bbb0-dc8287c37b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282786} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92feada-828f-5f43-8105-e8387a4f1f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2907fc18-ef26-5825-bc1f-b4cb4bdbf7cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167621" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-16$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-16 \\\\  & =16(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =32[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2157cbec-dd3c-50c6-8f66-e922ba1f66ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342514} {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b6665a-0ccb-5b28-87e4-9cb8ee2700ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi -1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342928} {:answer/id #uuid "a88c1662-aadb-5062-a115-55611ab6c159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343722} {:answer/id #uuid "bfef22ae-f97c-5172-89a1-1bc5619b881d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} :answer-field/id #uuid "09fe84d2-3042-5159-a1de-85adb6692d1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5649582e-ddb6-5db4-a7c3-699a007eaafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-76433" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7aeab2-63a6-55b6-9670-af11f7878d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342743} {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} {:answer/id #uuid "7df5078f-cc9c-5640-bdbe-c73ad5edaf2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343323} {:answer/id #uuid "d44736e7-181c-5708-8fc6-091f82bf604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344100} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc86749-0a58-547d-b292-dd0c15c33cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbabc5e-406f-59f6-a3fe-8b87b2e1d083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a51cb6-cac0-57a7-bccc-fcb3f00d4af4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76434" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=t-4.$     \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(4-t)}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}t\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec7f114-71dd-5707-a837-49ec4e0f58d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343037} {:answer/id #uuid "784dc8af-a9a4-53e0-8947-d481e7980aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af1a3-65b7-5d58-b8b9-b68cc63abf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343333} {:answer/id #uuid "e3af6d11-71f3-548a-854f-ff88ce98cd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b827e50e-d12a-5657-bed2-ba45d90ca8cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6c30ef5-4b00-5501-a5b6-18892cf4af06" :question/math-academy-id "q-76447" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4{t}^{2}$ and $y=3{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =8t\\,i+6t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =10|t| \\\\  & =10t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2{y}^{3}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(4{t}^{2})}^{3}-2{(3{t}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}{t}^{6}-2\\cdot {3}^{3}{t}^{6} \\\\  & =64{t}^{6}-54{t}^{6} \\\\  & =10{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{6}\\cdot 10t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =100{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{8}}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =100[\\frac{{1}^{8}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{8}}{8}] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133646 :question/id #uuid "e121d861-51e8-578a-9bbe-a3f11f9d9452" :question/math-academy-id "e-6316" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = xy \\,\\mathbf i - x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = t^3\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r (t))\\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = t^3, \\qquad y = t^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =xy\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{3})({t}^{2})\\,i-({t}^{3})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{5}\\,i-{t}^{6}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{5}\\,i-{t}^{6}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =3{t}^{7}-2{t}^{7} \\\\  & ={t}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e34a2cbf-0bed-50eb-9e62-7c6e55560cb9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2ca5b8-5981-551e-84c2-42df6d6e6ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257198} {:answer/id #uuid "553789d7-1249-5631-b43d-c24e6629b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257510} {:answer/id #uuid "92587863-834e-5824-84e8-490bc42faa0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-4{t}^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258073} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cc7618-4b33-520e-8e7b-a1238a933df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258674} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4592f9-7158-597f-b31d-611a63ed660a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "553789d7-1249-5631-b43d-c24e6629b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257510} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a824890-1de1-582c-a63f-8c8d8b7d97f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259188}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d06a9cd-a4fa-57a1-8abf-86189103f966" :question/math-academy-id "q-124638" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{2})^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & ={t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e8e2cc-41f8-50c1-a62f-8956a8ffa4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256812} {:answer/id #uuid "3acca87f-62d5-5a56-9a81-2550fd4f1695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257301} {:answer/id #uuid "50b95edd-6999-5bc4-882b-895dba85827e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257471} {:answer/id #uuid "9910c73f-0dfb-563e-8d27-612cfca9fe3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258131} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f0d5a5-db21-59c8-919e-c65d835eac2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3acca87f-62d5-5a56-9a81-2550fd4f1695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257301} :answer-field/id #uuid "d82657a2-f036-50a5-95af-ce80d925cc34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259412}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2bc899a-43d8-534f-8ee7-8f110107ecc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124687" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c76c281-ee32-5011-a748-7ef89ba883b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256901} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2f834c-7360-5d7e-aa9f-f3b1d66dc200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256926} {:answer/id #uuid "8deb98b1-da50-5ad9-81ca-a8b82c4d3504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258032} {:answer/id #uuid "f026e5a0-af76-56a7-9bbf-b3adf93f8cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258870} {:answer/id #uuid "f76be19f-342a-5624-9d5f-351998ae2615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8deb98b1-da50-5ad9-81ca-a8b82c4d3504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258032} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e2b23c-c022-536b-b1a7-a6999d60c1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34976ea5-f60a-5f5a-a35d-aa51a7c27ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124679" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(y+1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+(y+1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7aa7a8e-9e40-5890-b907-1646c2bfa769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387556} {:answer/id #uuid "fb04b673-cfa2-5b12-b9a4-5988d0fccddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387557} {:answer/id #uuid "031aa6d1-de81-500a-bcbc-17b2b9631f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387558} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a0a90d-b0de-571b-a337-5b1debe5a8d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}+\\sqrt{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387559} {:answer/id #uuid "95e15617-5463-564e-bb53-1c123c55a9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}+2\\sqrt{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb04b673-cfa2-5b12-b9a4-5988d0fccddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387557} :answer-field/id #uuid "77226689-1668-509a-b6a9-0092f1ff6932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387555}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dde4dac7-8853-5b97-ace9-e0b5cfb5ca89" :question/math-academy-id "q-124680" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=x{y}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+\\sqrt{t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x{y}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}{(\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t{e}^{t}\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}+2\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b27d22ad-8705-5f6a-aff8-aa84df701016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387563} {:answer/id #uuid "617b9542-8b66-5bd1-849d-8670a94817ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387564} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9766f3-a9ca-5758-a584-fb9fe4c46f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387565} {:answer/id #uuid "c46e8e1a-fb23-5e40-9a39-b39f7180ab64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387566} {:answer/id #uuid "057e9216-5ff0-599a-887d-e7e4c1f3cf25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e9766f3-a9ca-5758-a584-fb9fe4c46f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387565} :answer-field/id #uuid "055cc4a0-b3d1-567f-9dfb-6c6033f4d5df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387562}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8250ae2f-e76d-56bd-9c2b-33447c40d270" :question/math-academy-id "q-124685" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot ((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =t{cos}^{2}⁡t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of a Vector-Valued Functions Over Plane Curves"}]}
